{
 "method": "Voice-free stretch self-detected (300-3000 Hz 10 s block energy vs 10th pct); 1270-1780 s used as the quiet reference window. ILD = 10log10(E_L/E_R) on 250 ms hops of a band-passed pair. Interchannel delay via GCC-PHAT (4 s windows, 800-5000 Hz, parabolic peak interp) and independently via weighted least-squares slope of the cross-spectrum phase. Comb search: log-spectrum detrended with Savitzky-Golay then cepstrum/autocorrelation over 0.4-12 kHz. Coherent/incoherent split from |Pxy| vs sqrt(Pxx*Pyy). Comb/flanger hunt over the bed's own energy band, 150 Hz-2 kHz, on the noise CONTINUUM with every discrete line auto-masked (>8 dB over a 40 Hz local median, dilated +/-6 Hz; 1% of the band). Log-spectrum detrended with a 500 Hz Savitzky-Golay so any comb with delay >2 ms survives, then cepstrum, with an empirical null from 24 synthetic brown-noise realisations of identical length and identical processing. Long-window cepstral peaks DO clear the null (F10 at 8-10 ms, F12 at 2.5-20 ms), so the continuum is genuinely rougher than pure noise - but the decisive measurement is DEPTH, and every candidate delay from 2.5 to 30 ms yields only 0.1-0.8 dB peak-to-peak ripple. An audible flanger needs notches tens of dB deep. There is no flanger in any of the three tracks, in any band. Levels: bed_level_rms_rel_db = 20*log10( RMS of the tone-excised noise continuum over 20 Hz-20 kHz, averaged over L and R in power / RMS of the strongest single carrier sinusoid in the same window ). Carrier RMS = sqrt(integral of its PSD peak above the local median), i.e. A/sqrt(2) for a sine of amplitude A. Both from the same Welch PSD (2^17 pt, 0.34 Hz bins) on the same voice-free window.",
 "level_definition": "bed_level_rms_rel_db = 20*log10( RMS of the tone-excised noise continuum over 20 Hz-20 kHz, averaged over L and R in power / RMS of the strongest single carrier sinusoid in the same window ). Carrier RMS = sqrt(integral of its PSD peak above the local median), i.e. A/sqrt(2) for a sine of amplitude A. Both from the same Welch PSD (2^17 pt, 0.34 Hz bins) on the same voice-free window.",
 "files": "All three tape-era tracks (personal copies; durations match docs/tape-analysis/results.json, so its timeline offsets apply). The earlier F10 pass used a different, 80 s longer transfer of the same track; it is superseded by F10 here and the two agree.",
 "headline": "No flanger exists in any of the three tracks: notch depth is 0.1-0.8 dB p-p at every delay from 2.5 to 30 ms, in the bed's own 150 Hz-2 kHz energy band as well as 1.5-8 kHz. What F12 and F15 DO have is a shared ~9.7-9.8 s sinusoidal L/R pan of a fully decorrelated bed (coherence ~0.00), 3.3-4.5 dB peak-to-peak - a slow, wide, phasey drift that is the most likely source of the 'flanging' percept. Separately, the bed LEVELS are far higher than docs/tape-analysis/results.json implies: measured as RMS, F12's bed is +7.8 dB and F15's is +34.6 dB relative to their strongest carrier, where the old table said -14.1 and -30.3 dB. The old figures compared a single FFT bin of the bed against a carrier's whole coherent peak; the bed's energy is spread over ~20000 bins. That, not a missing flanger, is why the reproductions sound quiet.",
